WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.297
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.550
SPEAKER: A Google search, a
YouTube video, a connected Nest

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.500
device, and hundreds
of Google Cloud

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.500
services all give you a response
in a fraction of a second.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.660
But what really happens when you
click Search, send a request,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.520
or say, OK, Google.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.040
Well, your message travels
over the public internet

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.860
and into Google's
global fiber network.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.350
Millions of these requests,
or packets of data,

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.570
travel through miles
of cable over land

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.390
and under sea, converging
at one of the many data

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.820
centers we operate
all over the world.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.030
But besides delivering
quick results back to you,

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.560
what exactly is a data center?

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.370
A data center is a building
with powerful computers used

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.960
to run a company's services.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.840
It's where information is
processed and made available.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.240
Data is stored, managed,
and disseminated

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.470
across these computers.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.540
And network infrastructure
is installed

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.440
to support web apps, databases,
virtual machines, and more.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.800
Often, organizations start
with their own modest buildings

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.780
made up of a few
server racks, which

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.690
requires maintaining these
systems, paying for cooling,

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.060
and handling increasing demand.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.460
So many companies look
to a cloud services

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.290
provider like Google Cloud.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.760
These providers'
data centers have

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.240
evolved into large campuses
of multiple buildings.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.150
Hyperscale data centers,
made up of tens of thousands

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.630
of servers, process big data,
support cloud computing,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.820
and serve billions
of users for services

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.140
like Chrome, Maps, Gmail,
Search, and Google Cloud.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.060
In the cloud world,
companies and users

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.610
can benefit from this scale
because of a special thing

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.410
called multitenancy.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.290
This means customers can share
the resources of each server

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.440
in a data center, like tenants
sharing common resources

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.940
of an apartment building.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.880
Each tenant's cloud systems
are isolated and invisible

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.230
to other tenants.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.930
This means higher compute
processing and storage,

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.110
a more efficient use of data
center resources, and a much

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.440
lower cost than if you
managed your own servers.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.530
Google Cloud customers can even
deploy resources in regions

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.990
across the world, which
are enterprise data center

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.930
locations with
three or more zones.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.240
Zones are deployment areas that map to clusters of machines 

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.800
with distinct physical infrastructure. 

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.770
Companies can deploy workloads
across multiple zones

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.710
to help protect against
unexpected failures, something

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:21.440
you can't always get with a
single, private data center.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.280
It takes a right mix of energy,
cooling, and people to keep

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.780
a data center up and running.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.180
But it's a technical
feat Google's managed

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.350
to pull off to optimize for
efficiency, scale, and cost.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.050
More on that next time on
"Discovering Data Centers."

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

